<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  crsB arm parameters — SYSID set.

  The identified set, taken verbatim from the accepted model of an
  offline system-identification run against the physical G2 (model
  dated 2026-09-22), bodies arm_{l,r}_link1..7:
    <inertial mass= pos= fullinertia=>  -> mass / com_xyz / inertia
    <joint damping= frictionloss=>      -> damping / friction
  Schema mirrors G2.arm.crsB.params.default.xacro so
  `diff G2.arm.crsB.params.default.xacro G2.arm.crsB.params.sysid.xacro`
  reads as a pure spreadsheet of number changes.  Velocity limits are
  carried over from the default file unchanged — the run has nothing to
  say about them.

  SUPERSEDES the earlier T2 v2 set that occupied this filename (added
  in commit d440207856).  That set ran ~4.8% heavy on every link,
  placed several COMs up to 6 cm from CAD, and was not bilaterally
  consistent — its own header asked for symmetrization before shipping.
  The numbers remain recoverable from git at that commit.

  WHAT THIS SET DOES AND DOES NOT CARRY

  The run declares no actuator model, so it supplies no Kp/Kd and no
  torque constant.  The <drive stiffness= damping=> gains in
  G2.arm.crsB.urdf.xacro are the sim's own and are left untouched.
  Armature has no URDF representation at all; the identified armature
  values live in robots/genie/g2/g2_crsB_drive_params.sysid.yaml
  alongside the same damping/frictionloss numbers.

  TRANSFER IS DIRECT, AND THAT WAS CHECKED

  The source model is MJCF, where `fullinertia` is the COM inertia in
  the body frame, in the order ixx iyy izz ixy ixz iyz — exactly URDF's
  <inertia> with rpy="0 0 0".  The body frames coincide with these link
  frames: joint positions match to 0.0, joint ranges to 0.0,
  orientation quaternions to 4.8e-7, and every joint axis is (0,0,1) on
  both sides.  CAD's link1 COM and the identified one agree to 8.5e-8
  m, independently confirming the frames.  So the numbers transfer
  componentwise with no rotation.  (Note the default file expresses
  link3 and link7 in principal axes via a nonzero com_rpy; this file
  uses the body frame throughout, so ixy/ixz/iyz are nonzero here.)

  WHAT CHANGED VS CAD

  Masses: links 1-3 equal CAD to 1e-10 kg (2.17 / 1.90 / 1.86) — those
  are frozen, not recovered.  Links 4-7 come down 3.1% to 9.5%,
  negative on both arms: l4 -3.1%, l5 -3.2%, l6 -9.3%, l7 -3.6%;
  r4 -4.1%, r5 -5.9%, r6 -9.5%, r7 -3.6%.  Total moving arm mass
  9.358 -> 9.198 kg (left) / 9.152 kg (right).

  Friction and damping are a much larger change than the inertials.
  frictionloss goes from the sim's 1.0/1.0/0.5/0.5/0.5/0.2/0.2 N*m to
  5.25/5.37/1.85/1.63/1.34/1.98/1.54 (left) — 3-5x on the shoulder and
  ~8x on the wrist.  Joint damping goes from 0 (the attribute was
  absent) to 4.16/2.78/1.27/1.95/0.60/0.64/0.88 N*m*s/rad.  If
  something gets noticeably heavier to move after switching, this is
  why, not the masses.

  CAVEATS — these are an identification result, not measurements

    * The link masses are not identifiable from the current data.  The
      pure-mass regressor columns for L2/L3 carry a torque derivative
      of ~7e-10 N*m/kg; with a weaker prior left L1 ranges over
      3.31-4.10 kg for a training-error difference of ~0.021 mA.  The
      masses here are prior- and freeze-dominated.  Their bilateral
      consistency is the signature of regularization having been added
      after the previous revision produced sign-inconsistent arms
      (l4 -42% vs r4 +22%, l5 +30% vs r5 -34%), not of independent
      agreement.
    * The run's training data provenance is unknown and may overlap our
      holdout recordings, so its good holdout scores are not
      independent validation.  With inertials left free, all three
      candidate references (CAD, this set, and a mapped reference from
      a comparable arm) converge to the same ~50.3 / 54.9 mA training
      residual — the data does not distinguish them.  What this set
      does win on, as a fixed reference: 1 s forward median 3.045 deg
      vs CAD's 3.324 (left), and friction holdout R2 0.7399 vs 0.7255
      (right).
    * Right L3's mass distribution does not fit inside the collision
      shell: directional variance along body z is 0.01692 m2 against a
      mesh support bound of 0.01232 m2, 1.37x over.  That assumes the
      mesh holds all of the link's mass, but no other reference in the
      comparison failed this way.  This set's L3 and L5 are also far
      "wider" than the mapped reference arm's corresponding links
      (widest RMS 106/130 mm vs 44 mm, and 75/60 mm vs 35 mm).
    * damping and frictionloss were NOT refit in this revision — they
      are held at the previous revision's values (identical to 4
      decimals on all 14 joints).  Only the inertials were revised;
      armature moved <=4.1%.
    * The run's low-speed friction basis zeroes F below |v| < 0.02
      rad/s, and 42.2% of the J2 friction training points fall in that
      band without being stationary.  A 0.002 threshold cuts the
      friction training RMSE from 3.15-3.17 to 1.11-1.22 N*m (left).
      The shoulder friction numbers above are the ones most affected.
    * The run's own summary figures disagree with its accepted model by
      more than rounding, so the two are separate sources; this file
      follows the model.

  All 14 of these inertials are physically consistent — min eig of
  Sigma = tr(I_C)/2*I - I_C is 1.0e-4 to 1.2e-3, so each one is a
  realizable rigid body.  The superseded T2 v2 set was marginally
  non-PSD on r3/r4/r5/r7 (~-6e-10, i.e. at its own 9-decimal rounding).

  Runtime status: this selector supplies assembly inertials and URDF
  dynamics. Compatible bringup scenes also select robot.joint_overlays;
  Channel B then applies identified per-joint passive values after class
  fallbacks in Isaac PhysX 6.2 and Newton MuJoCo-Warp. The xacro selector
  alone does not enable runtime overlays; see robots/SYSID_PARAMS.md.

  Selection: `xacro ... inertia_source:=sysid`.
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

